reat but if you think about once  you scan a barcode what all things need  to happen before it actually shows up on  the screen it need to fetch item  information and it need to calculate the  price it needs to incorporate the  promotion's it need to calculate tags  before it actually shows up there so  I'll need to happen in within 250  milliseconds so we probably have a  different kind of issue to solve from a  response standpoint right so those are  two reasons why we probably need  computer on the edge because we just  can't afford to take the round trip back  to cloud or tornado center it's a bit of  history how we operated computer in the  stores if you look at five years and  back we bundle things right application  developers build things and they get  bundled together tested most times by a  separate team there is a deployment team  that actually did the deployment there  is a huge orchestration around it  because these deployments are huge and  they cost a store outage right so and  that's disruptive to the stores so you  need to find out what's a good time to  deploy this and that required a whole  team of people to actually manage it how  many other retailers this sounds  familiar so so we were there so and then  around you know 2015 2016 2016 is time  period of course we started embracing  open source we have the Linux server  available in the store and so people  were able to start building  micro-service is great right and then of  course we use you know team started  using shop cook books to start deploying  right it's great and some of the  challenges like ethan mentioned earlier  related to how people build cookbooks  and how it becomes complicated it  it's started becoming challenging for  the teams they were able to deploy it  fairly successfully but it's more like  80% right  you still need to login to a sufficient  set of stores to fix things  it soon became evident that teams were  spending like sometimes up to 70% of  their Dow the developers